.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-SET-BUCKET-PRIORITY 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-set-bucket-priority \- set the priority a bucket's notifications are sent with
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm set-bucket-priority
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-priority | \-p
.IR LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets the priority the notifications this bucket sends are given.
.PP
.B The bucket does nothing with it.
A bucket is not consumed from and has no queue of its own, so there is
nothing here for a priority to mean. It exists only to be handed on \(em
to the messages a subscription of this bucket turns an object event into.
"Everything that lands in this bucket is urgent" is the statement it
makes, and the queue on the other side of the subscription is where that
statement finally has an effect.
.PP
It reaches a queue by either route a bucket subscription takes: directly,
for an
.I SQS
subscription, and through the topic for an
.IR SNS
one \(em see
.BR euclid-cli-esm-subscribe (1).
Both end at the same place, and it keeps the same standing on both.
.SH WHICH PRIORITY WINS
Four statements can be in play about one message. Least specific first,
each overriding the one before it:
.TP
.B 1. the queue's own default
Set when the queue was created. This is the floor, and it is also where
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-send-message (1)
starts from, so a message put into a queue by a subscription and one put
there by a client are treated alike.
.TP
.B 2. the bucket's priority
What this command sets. A statement about every object in the bucket.
.TP
.B 3. the object's own
A
.I priority
in the object's system attributes, set by whatever wrote it. A statement
about one object, so it beats the bucket's \(em which is what lets a
bucket set a floor without taking away the ability to say more about a
particular object.
.TP
.B 4. a priority the message already had
What a topic hop carries. Most specific, because it is not a default at
all.
.SH EMPTY IS NOT MEDIUM
An empty
.B \-\-priority
clears it, and that is the only way back to letting the queue decide.
.PP
The distinction matters: a bucket that says nothing leaves a queue created
with
.I LOW
delivering at
.IR LOW ,
where a bucket saying
.I MEDIUM
would override it. Every bucket that existed before buckets could carry a
priority says nothing, and behaves exactly as it did.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME\fR
Bucket to change. A name is enough; a full ERN also works. Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-priority " " \fILOW\fR|\fIMEDIUM\fR|\fIHIGH\fR
The priority. Case is not significant \(em it is stored as the upper-case
spelling a queue and a message use. Omit it, or pass an empty string, to
clear it. Anything that is not one of the three is refused rather than
ignored, so a bucket cannot end up looking configured and behaving as
though it were not.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The priority was set, and the bucket is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, an unrecognised priority, no such bucket,
the bucket belongs to another account, the caller was not granted it, or
the server could not be reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
Everything arriving in this bucket is urgent:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli esm set\-bucket\-priority \-\-bucket inbox \-\-priority HIGH
.fi
.RE
.PP
And back to letting each target queue decide:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli esm set\-bucket\-priority \-\-bucket inbox \-\-priority ""
.fi
.RE
.PP
Set at creation instead:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli esm create\-bucket \-\-name inbox \-\-priority HIGH
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-create-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-subscribe (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-send-message (1)
